{
  "id": "ch5-no-a6",
  "title": "When Black leaves out 3...a6",
  "intro": "Black does not ask the bishop the question, so White answers it anyway. Take on c6 and play the structure you already know.\n\n## The rule for the whole chapter\n\nBxc6 in every line here. It costs a move of development compared with the lines where Black has played ...a6, and it buys the same crippled queenside and the same healthy majority. One structure understood beats five structures half known, and that is the whole argument for choosing the Exchange.\n\n## The Berlin, 3...Nf6\n\nThe one that matters, because it is much the most common. 4.Bxc6 dxc6 5.O-O and Black usually grabs on e4. Let him. 6.Re1 puts the rook on the file the knight is standing in, and after 6...Nd6 7.Nxe5 the pawn is back with the structure fixed. The trap to know is 7...Qe7, which looks like it wins a piece: 8.Nxf7 steps aside and the rook on the e-file collects the queen. The other line worth its own look is 5...Bd6 6.d4 exd4, where 7.e5 forks the bishop and the knight on f6.\n\n## The rest\n\n3...Bc5 invites the wrong recapture: after 4.Bxc6 bxc6 5.Nxe5 is a clean pawn, because the bishop on c5 does not defend it and there is no ...Qd4 with the d-pawn at home.\n\n3...d6 is met with check, so Black has no choice: 4.Bxc6+ bxc6 5.d4, which is the 4...bxc6 chapter with a move saved.\n\n3...f5 is the only line where taking on e5 is punished by a pawn rather than a piece. Take on c6, then defend e4 with a piece: 5.Nc3.\n\n3...g6 and 3...Nge7 are slow. Take, build with d3 and Nbd2 and Nc4, and let the structure be the difference. The knight tour against 3...g6 sets a small trap of its own: once it lands on c4, e5 is attacked twice and defended once, and the count says take.\n\n## One honest note\n\nAgainst 3...Nge7 taking on c6 is not White's most testing move: the engine puts 4.Nc3 close to a pawn ahead. This course takes anyway, and says so rather than pretending. The position after it is a comfortable game in the structure the trainee knows.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nTaking on e5 before counting what defends it. Grabbing on e5 against the Schliemann while e4 hangs. Forgetting that the capture is check against 3...d6. And treating the Berlin as a different opening: after 4.Bxc6 dxc6 it is this one.",
